<!--
  IL2CPP code stripping removes managed methods nothing in managed code calls.

  That describes every callback here exactly: JoryioCallbackReceiver.OnInAppMessage
  is invoked BY NAME from native through UnitySendMessage, so the stripper sees an
  unreferenced private method and deletes it.

  The failure mode this guards against: stripping is off in development builds,
  so a stripped callback works throughout QA and stops working in the release
  build - on device, with no exception, looking like a server or device problem
  rather than a build-configuration one.

  MEASURED on Unity 6000.4: an iOS export with IL2CPP and stripping level High
  generates the callback bodies even with this file and every [Preserve]
  removed, because Unity is conservative about MonoBehaviour methods that
  SendMessage could invoke by name. So this is insurance, not load-bearing
  today. It stays because the stripper's behaviour is not a contract - a higher
  stripping level, a newer Unity, or moving the callbacks off a MonoBehaviour
  would each remove what is protecting them. Re-run the export before deleting
  it.
-->
<linker>
  <assembly fullname="Joryio.Runtime" preserve="all"/>
</linker>
